WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.107
This slide introduces two common import patterns: aliases and selective imports.

00:00:06.107 --> 00:00:10.402
An alias gives a module a shorter or more familiar name.

00:00:10.402 --> 00:00:13.762
For example, many Python programmers write import pandas as pd.

00:00:13.762 --> 00:00:17.882
That means we can type pd instead of pandas.

00:00:17.882 --> 00:00:22.602
The alias is helpful because it is short and widely recognized.

00:00:22.602 --> 00:00:25.872
In this slide, we use import statistics as stats.

00:00:25.872 --> 00:00:31.452
This means the statistics module is available using the shorter name stats.

00:00:31.452 --> 00:00:34.322
Selective imports work differently.

00:00:34.322 --> 00:00:41.642
When we write from datetime import date, we are importing only the date part from the datetime module.

00:00:41.642 --> 00:00:45.757
Then we can use date directly instead of writing datetime.date.

00:00:45.757 --> 00:00:50.322
There is no single import style that is always best.

00:00:50.322 --> 00:00:57.492
If you need many tools from a module, importing the whole module can be clearer.

00:00:57.492 --> 00:01:00.807
If a short alias is common and recognizable, an alias can improve readability.

00:01:00.807 --> 00:01:09.307
If you only need one specific function or class, a selective import can be convenient.

00:01:09.307 --> 00:01:14.632
The most important rule is this: use imports in a way that helps the reader understand where each

00:01:14.632 --> 00:01:20.560
tool comes from. Do not create confusing nicknames just to make the code shorter.
